      "role": "user",
      "content": "this is perfect—it's exactly that layered friction i was looking for. the idea of Captain Chen being seen as \"too polished\" and Aisha being viewed as a potential informant just by the nature of her position is exactly the kind of realism that makes a drama work.\n\ni want to push this even further into a specific scene to see if we can capture that tension in dialogue. let's set a scene in a cramped, dimly lit local diner near the precinct. \n\ncould you write a short script snippet (maybe 6-8 lines of dialogue total) involving Detective Hayes and Hector? i want it to show that immediate, unsaid tension. Hayes is trying to get information about a recent incident, and Hector is using that \"obstructionist\" but respectful authority you mentioned. don't make them shouting; keep it subtle, using the subtext of their different backgrounds and how they're \"read\" by each other to show that they're essentially speaking two different languages even when they're using the same words.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
